.5, "sigma_total": 5.87, "baseline_margin": 16.03, "analyst_adjustment": -0.03, "key_factors": ["Pritzker is a well-funded incumbent in a state leaning Democratic by six points", "All eight expert ratings call the race Solid or Safe Democratic", "Recent polls show Pritzker ahead by 18 points or more", "Prediction markets price Pritzker at 98-99% to win"], "rationale": "Every expert rating puts the race in the Solid or Safe Democratic column, late polls show Pritzker up roughly 18 points, and markets price his win probability near 99%. The fundamentals baseline of about 13 points plus incumbency supports a double-digit margin, so the forecast stays at 16 points with modest uncertainty.", "watch": "A sharp late shift on affordability could tighten the race, but Bailey would need a historic polling miss to threaten Pritzker's third term.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent JB Pritzker (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Governor JB Pritzker seeks a rare third term in a 2022 rematch against former state Senator Darren Bailey. Illinois leans Democratic by about six points, and Pritzker enters with the advantages of incumbency, deep personal resources, and a favorable national midterm map for his party in this state. Every major race rating, from Cook Political Report to Sabato's Crystal Ball, places the contest in the Solid or Safe Democratic column.\n\nLate polls reinforce that picture: ActiVote surveys in late October show Pritzker ahead 59-41, and earlier polls from Public Policy Polling and Victory Research showed a 20-point margin. Prediction markets on Kalshi and Polymarket price Pritzker's win probability at 98 to 99 percent. The campaign's final days have centered on affordability, but no evidence suggests the race is competitive.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.98, "margin": 13.88}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9829, "margin": 14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.983, "margin": 14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9789, "margin